## std::fixed — Fixed-Capacity Collections (core quality)
## ArrayVec / RingBuffer / SpscQueue — inline storage, no allocator, no locks,
## ownership-transferring push/pop with exact structural drop.
##
## Spec: P1-17 (fixed-capacity collections promoted to core quality) and
## P1-21 (the fixed-capacity lock-free SPSC queue with split endpoints).
##
## ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
## COMPILE-TIME CAPACITY — the concrete families and the const-generic
## views (DECIDED, documented)
## ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
## The file carries TWO compile-time-capacity surfaces over ONE slot
## model and ONE ownership contract:
##
## 1. The CONCRETE-FIXED-STRUCT families — ArrayVec4[T]..ArrayVec64[T],
##    RingBuffer4[T]..RingBuffer64[T], and the split-endpoint
##    SpscQueueN[T] / SpscTxN[T] / SpscRxN[T] (N = 4..32) — the original
##    core-quality form. They PREDATE the language's const generics
##    (§20: Type::Const / Type::ConstParam / Type::ConstSym, value-level
##    reads of the parameter in generic bodies, impl-level const
##    parameters) and remain in the file as the ENGINE and for
##    stage0-subset compatibility: the capacity is part of the TYPE NAME
##    (a compile-time fact carried by the type identity), the storage is
##    a direct inline [Option[T]; N] field of the struct, and no element
##    type constraint (no T: Copy, no T: Default) is imposed — the
##    promotion over the std::embedded prototypes. Every concrete
##    RingBuffer/SpscQueue capacity is a power of two (the ring indexes
##    with the mask (i + 1) & (N - 1) fast path). Unchanged by the
##    generic-view additions below.
##
## 2. The CONST-GENERIC VIEWS — ArrayVec[T, const N: UInt] and
##    RingBuffer[T, const N: UInt] — the PROMOTED surface for new code:
##    ONE declaration over the capacity parameter, so user code writes
##    ArrayVec[T, 8] where the engine spells ArrayVec8[T]. A view
##    declaration carries the SAME body the concrete families do, with
##    the capacity as the const parameter:
##
##        struct ArrayVec[T, const N: UInt]
##          _data: [Option[T]; N]    # Type::FixedArraySym while generic
##          _len: UInt
##        end
##
##    An `[Option[T]; N]` field whose N is the declaration's own const
##    parameter typechecks symbolically in the generic body (§20 slice 1
##    — the canary arrayvec pattern), instantiations are ONE canonical
##    type per capacity (ArrayVec[Int, 4] and ArrayVec[Int, 8] are
##    different types; every spelling of the same size is one type), and
##    the parameter is readable as a per-instance VALUE in the generic
##    bodies (§20 slice 3): capacity() returns N, is_full compares
##    against N, and the ring wraps through the computed (N - 1) mask —
##    no literal mask constants. Method bodies lower once; the concrete
##    instance substitutes per capacity, and the drop glue over the
##    monomorphized [Option[T]; N] storage is the SAME structural glue
##    the concrete families use (occupied Some slots destructed exactly
##    once at end of life).
##
## CAPACITY VALIDATION: RingBuffer's mask fast path is sound only when
## the capacity is a POWER OF TWO — the generic RingBuffer[T, N] wrap is
## (i + 1) & (N - 1), the wrap-around mask exactly when N is a power of
## two. The requirement is enforced at construction: the view's new()
## asserts N is a power of two at runtime and panics otherwise (the
## construction-time runtime guard). The concrete family capacities are
## powers of two by construction (the type names). ArrayVec accepts any
## N >= 0.
##
## CONSTRUCTION: the view new()s write the N-item Option::None fill through
## the [elem; count] REPEAT-ARRAY literal whose count is the const
## parameter ([Option::None; N] — the grammar's repeat form, lowered to the
## MIR MirRepeat slot-fill with the count as the per-instance value read of
## N; the element is the payload-less None image, so the fill is sound for
## every element type T — a None slot's payload is never read or dropped).
## Before the repeat lowering existed, values of the view types were
## constructed AT CONCRETE SITES through the §20 aggregate-literal bridge
## (an array literal may initialize the symbolic-size field, the item count
## enforced at the substituted destination) or from the concrete engine
## family; that spelling remains legal. Every OTHER view method (len /
## capacity / is_empty / is_full / push / pop / clear and the Copy-gated
## get / peek) is a generic body over the existing value and mirrors the
## concrete families' logic exactly with N read as a value.
##
## The SPSC queue HAS the const-generic family here: SpscQueue[T, N] /
## SpscTx[T, N] / SpscRx[T, N] express with const N exactly like the views
## (the record's [Option[T]; N] buffer substitutes per instance), and the
## queue's ONLY construction path writes the shared record through the same
## N-item None fill, so new() is the §20 mirror of the concrete families.
## SpscQueue4..32 remain the engine.
##
## ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
## SLOT MODEL — why the storage is [Option[T]; N] (DECIDED, documented)
## ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
## The kernel's destruction protocol is the compiler-generated drop glue
## over the STRUCT SHAPE (the deinit plans + the pre-generated glue
## functions walk the concrete type; Type::FixedArray(elem, _) glue walks
## EVERY element of the inline array). A raw `[T; N]` field of an owning
## element type therefore cannot hold lazily-initialized slots — the glue
## would destruct never-initialized memory. The slot-valid invariant is
## the Option form: every slot is ALWAYS a valid `Option[T]` value
## (`None` at rest). The structural glue is then EXACT:
##   - occupied slots (Some) are destructed exactly once, by the glue, at
##     the container's end of life;
##   - vacant slots are the None discriminant — their glue is trivial.
## No custom `deinit`/`Drop` exists on the inline containers (ArrayVec,
## RingBuffer): owning fields are dropped structurally — the kernel's
## deinit convention (a struct that owns VALUES holds them structurally
## and declares no finalizer; a struct that owns MEMORY holds a raw
## pointer and declares the finalizer — see std::alloc.tg Box/Arc and
## std::collections.tg SliceBacking).
##
## "No initialization of unused storage" is satisfied in the semantic
## sense: no element T is EVER constructed for an unused slot (no
## T::default, no element constructor, no per-slot allocation). The only
## per-slot write at construction is the Option None DISCRIMINANT
## (a plain tag image; Option::None carries no T). This is the kernel's
## exact-drop boundary: the None discriminant is what makes the slot
## glue-trivial, so writing it is REQUIRED by the glue-exactness rule
## above — raw maybe-uninit slots are not representable in a struct whose
## glue the compiler generates.
##
## Construction spell (engine families): `[Option::None, ...]` — an
## explicit AGGREGATE array literal of N None variants (aggregate
## construction is the sanctioned ownership-transfer point in the MIR; the
## item count of an array literal is concrete, spelled at concrete capacity
## sites). Each capacity struct's `new()` writes exactly N None items.
## The GENERIC VIEWS write the same fill through the [elem; count] REPEAT
## literal ([Option::None; N]): the count is the const parameter read as
## a per-instance value (the MirRepeat count operand, substituted by
## monomorphization), and the repeated element is the payload-less None
## image — a tag-only constant, so a None slot's payload bytes are never
## read or dropped under ANY element type T (the exact-drop boundary is
## unchanged: the None discriminant is what makes a slot glue-trivial).
##
## ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
## PUSH/POP OWNERSHIP CONTRACT
## ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
##   push(inout self, sink item) -> Result[Unit, T]
##     Consumes the item INTO the container. When the container is full
##     the item was NOT consumed and is returned to the caller as
##     Result::Err(item) — the sink-rejected-value full policy.
##   pop(inout self) -> Option[T]
##     Transfers ownership of the last/front element OUT of the container
##     (the slot is reset to None; the caller owns the returned value).
##   Element slot stores target slots that are vacant by construction
##   (the len/count discipline), so the store's old-value policy — the
##   MIR emits no old-element drop inside generic bodies (deferred
##   element type) and the concrete glue drop on the monomorphized
##   instances — is safe under BOTH policies: the overwritten value is
##   always a None.
##
## ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
## THE SPSC QUEUE — shared state and the ordering surface (DECIDED)
## ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
## SpscQueueN[T] is the shared state record; SpscQueueN::new() splits it
## into the two UNIQUE endpoints (SpscTxN[T], SpscRxN[T]) — tx pushes
## only, rx pops only. Unique endpoint ownership is enforced by the
## registered Drop on both endpoints: the types are not trivially
## copyable and expose no clone, so an endpoint can only be MOVED
## (including across a thread boundary; see the UnsafeTransferable gate
## impls below).
##
## The endpoints must be usable from two threads at once, so the state is
## ONE shared heap record — a single allocation for the whole queue (the
## queue data itself lives INLINE in the record's slot array; there is no
## per-element allocation, no lock, no Arc per element, no Arc at all).
## The split-endpoint ownership model makes one shared allocation
## unavoidable: two independently-owned values cannot embed one inline
## buffer. Lifetime is handled by the count-2 protocol (no clones are
## possible, so the count is exactly 2 -> 1 -> 0): the endpoint that
## observes the 2 -> 1 transition leaves the record alive for the other
## endpoint; the endpoint that observes the 1 -> 0 transition runs the
## record's typed drop glue (drop_in_place — every occupied slot
## destructed exactly once by the structural glue) and frees the
## allocation. A producer can never push into freed state: the record is
## only freed by the LAST endpoint's own drop.
##
## The atomics use the KERNEL's atomic surface — the __sync builtin
## family on plain UInt fields, exactly as std::alloc.tg's Rc/Arc
## refcounts do (std::atomic is outside the self-hosted kernel closure,
## so the __sync family is the kernel's one atomic layer). Every access
## to a shared word is a __sync operation (no plain read or write of a
## shared word anywhere):
##   - the head word is the producer's publication state: the published
##     producer counter in bits [0, 63) AND the CLOSED bit at bit 63 —
##     one word, so the publish decision and the close decision are one
##     atomic compare-and-swap apart (the closed state cannot slip into
##     the window between a close flag and a publish RMW);
##   - publication (producer): slot write, THEN the full-barrier CAS
##     (open, H) -> (open, H + 1) on the head word — the CAS success is
##     the linearization point of the push and publishes the element;
##   - publication (consumer): slot take, THEN __sync_fetch_and_add on
##     the tail counter — the full-barrier RMW publishes the free slot;
##   - the far-side reads (head by rx, tail by tx) are
##     __sync_fetch_and_add(x, 0) loads.
## The __sync ops are sequentially consistent (strictly stronger than
## the Release/Acquire minimum the SPSC protocol needs) — the kernel's
## __sync surface exposes no narrower ordering primitive (the UInt CAS
## is __sync_val_compare_and_swap_uint), so the publication boundaries
## carry the full-barrier form. The head counter (low 63 bits) and the
## tail counter are monotonic; the lead (head - tail) is the occupancy
## and the slot index is (counter & (N - 1)) — every capacity here is a
## power of two, and the mask-derived index is untouched by the packed
## CLOSED bit (bit 63 needs no realignment of the slot index).
## COUNTER BOUND: the head counter can never reach bit 63 — the CLOSED
## bit lives above 2^63 - 1 published items. Even at one push per
## nanosecond the counter would need ~292 years of continuous pushing to
## collide, and every push requires a slot that only a pop frees, so the
## bound is unreachable in practice (2^63 - 1 is the documented cap).
##   - tx.push probes the head word once: CLOSED set -> the receiver
##     endpoint has been dropped and the queue accepts nothing;
##     Result::Err(item) without touching a slot (the fast path for the
##     closed queue). Open: the full check (head_state - tail >= N ->
##     Err(item)), then the candidate slot write, then the publish CAS.
##     A CAS failure is the SINGLE authority for "closed": only the
##     receiver's close touches the head word beside this producer, so
##     (open, H) can only have changed by the CLOSED bit — the just
##     written slot never became observable, it is reclaimed (the value
##     taken back) and the item returns as Err. The push therefore
##     returns Ok IFF its item became observable before the close
##     linearization — the close semantic is LINEARIZABLE, no residual
##     Ok-after-close window exists. (The pre-probe is a pure fast path
##     that keeps closed queues slot-free; the CAS failure covers the
##     close that lands between the probe and the publish, and the probe
##     is NOT re-checked — the CAS result is authoritative.)
##   - receiver close (rx.drop): OR the CLOSED bit into the head word
##     with a fetch_or-style CAS loop (load; if CLOSED already set the
##     close is idempotent and done; else CAS (cur -> cur | CLOSED),
##     retrying when a producer publish won the CAS in between). The
##     successful CAS IS the linearization point of close: every
##     producer CAS that succeeded before it published an observable
##     item (Ok), every producer CAS after it fails and reclaims (Err).
##   - rx.pop reads the head word and MASKS the CLOSED bit off (the
##     word may carry it in every state the protocol can reach); when
##     head == tail the queue is empty and pop returns None (pop NEVER
##     blocks — a closed queue is drained by pop exactly like an open
##     one, and rx.pop only runs while the receiver endpoint lives).
## len() reads the far-side counter (masked on the rx side) and is
## exact at the load instant (the publication order makes a load either
## before or after the far side's full-barrier publish).
##
## DROP DISCIPLINE under the packed scheme: rx.drop first ORs the
## CLOSED bit into the head word, then decrements the count; tx.drop
## decrements the count. Whichever endpoint drops last runs the
## record's drop_in_place and dealloc. The glue can only run at the
## count 1 -> 0 transition, which requires BOTH endpoint values to be
## gone — no push is in flight then, so a candidate slot write can
## never race the glue. Every item is destroyed exactly once: Ok items
## were published and are destructed by the glue (or popped); Err items
## return to the caller; a reclaimed slot is None again before any glue
## can run. Producer pushes into a closed queue are safe because the
## record is freed only by the last endpoint's drop, never while this
## producer's push call is alive.

# Module identity: std::fixed (the file-based module — same convention as
# std/collections.tg: no `module` block; the file name is the module path).
use std::core::{Option, Result, Unit, Bool, UInt, panic}
use std::alloc::{alloc, dealloc}

# [METADATA]
# module: tg::fixed
# status: alpha
# effects: []
# capabilities: []


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# ArrayVec — fixed-capacity vector (inline [Option[T]; N] storage)
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# push consumes the item (Result::Err(item) on full — the rejected value
# returns to the caller); pop transfers ownership; clear drains and drops
# the occupied slots through pop; the drop glue over the inline Option
# slots destructs exactly the occupied elements at end of life.

## Fixed-capacity vector, 4 elements.
struct ArrayVec4[T]
  _data: [Option[T]; 4]
  _len: UInt
end

impl[T] ArrayVec4[T]
  def new() -> ArrayVec4[T]
    ArrayVec4 {
      _data: [
        Option::None, Option::None, Option::None, Option::None
      ],
      _len: 0
    }
  end

  def len(self: Self) -> UInt
    self._len
  end

  def capacity(self: Self) -> UInt
    4
  end

  def is_empty(self: Self) -> Bool
    self._len == 0
  end

  def is_full(self: Self) -> Bool
    self._len >= 4
  end

  # Ownership-taking push: consumes the item into slot [_len] (vacant by
  # the len discipline). On a full vector the item was NOT consumed and is
  # returned to the caller as Err(item).
  def push(inout self: Self, sink item: T) -> Result[Unit, T]
    if self.is_full() then
      return Result::Err(item)
    end
    self._data[self._len] = Option::Some(item)
    self._len = self._len + 1
    Result::Ok(())
  end

  # Ownership-transferring pop: the last occupied slot's Option is taken
  # (the slot resets to None) and the element moves out to the caller.
  def pop(inout self: Self) -> Option[T]
    if self._len == 0 then
      return Option::None
    end
    self._len = self._len - 1
    self._data[self._len].take()
  end

  # Drains every occupied slot through pop (each element destructed by
  # its own drop as the returned Option is discarded — the VecDeque::clear
  # convention in std::collections.tg).
  def clear(inout self: Self) -> Unit
    while self._len > 0 do
      self.pop()
    end
  end
end

# By-value projected read of a Copy element (slot Option[T] is copied
# only under T: Copy — sound duplication).
impl[T: Copy] ArrayVec4[T]
  def get(self: Self, index: UInt) -> Option[T]
    if index >= self._len then
      return Option::None
    end
    match self._data[index]
    when Option::Some(v) then Option::Some(v)
    when Option::None then Option::None
    end
  end
end

